re  where the government sets the terms and  says trade surpluses and growth  industries matter now you the free  market sector get on with it as far as  shareholder democracy and shareholders  I do support shareholder democracy  absolutely and the shareholding system  but we do have to be aware that there's  more and more pressure on corporations  to be shorter and shorter term and China  is gaining on us quickly and when we  look at for instance the steel industry  China is now more than 50 percent of  world's steel production there are key  elements in our military I'm talking  about ships and planes and vehicles  where the components cannot be  manufactured here then we need to import  them and if you take a long term view it  is possible there will be a war again  somewhere someday and the government and  the private sector need to be aware of  that and take a long term view with  China we have an adversary in the world  that's a different kind of nation from  previous adversaries I'll just mention a  joke some of you may know Deng Chao ping  who basically invented the current  Chinese system which is capitalism in  communist clothes was asked once back in  the 1990s whether he thought the French  Revolution was a success he thought for  a moment and his answer was it's a  little too soon to tell I think that  expresses how the Chinese view the  challenge of the 21st century and I  think it's incumbent upon us to take  that kind of view as well thank you Jeff  please join me in thanking our panel  [Applause]
 

